<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liberius (praetorian prefect)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 465–<a href="/years/554">554</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Petrus Marcellinus Felix Liberius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liberius was a Roman aristocrat and official who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a>, the Ostrogothic king Theodoric and finally the emperor Justinian. He was praetorian prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and later governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for Justinian. In 552, already in his eighties, he led the expedition that landed in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and began the Byzantine province of Spania.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The origin of Liberius is unknown, though it has been suggested that he came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a>. He belonged to a senatorial family, not one of the most prominent. He was married to Agretia, possibly his second wife, and had at least two sons and a daughter; his son Venantius was consul in 507. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> deposed Romulus Augustus in 476 the Roman administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> went on working, staffed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and the young Liberius served it as other senatorial families did.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 493, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a>'s murder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> made Liberius praetorian prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He held the office until 500, handling finances and the settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, and his contemporaries Magnus Felix Ennodius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus">Cassiodorus</a> praised him. In 510 Theodoric revived the prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, seated at Arelatum, to govern newly conquered Provence, and Liberius held it for more than two decades, with the help of Bishop Caesarius. In the mid-520s he was stabbed in the abdomen during a Visigoth raid. He built a new cathedral at Orange, where the Second Council of Orange met in 529, and his signature heads its lay endorsers.</p><p>After Theodahad imprisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalasuntha">Amalasuntha</a>, the regent of the Ostrogothic Kingdom, Liberius and Venantius Opilio were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> with a mild account of events, but at Avlona they told the emperor's envoy Peter what had really happened. Liberius stayed in Constantinople. Around 538 Justinian made him Augustal Prefect of Egypt to suppress the Monophysites; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> describes a troubled tenure, and in 542 he defended his conduct before a senatorial inquiry. In 550 he led an army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and entered besieged Syracuse, but achieved little and was replaced at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> in 551 by the Armenian general Artabanes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In May 553 Liberius took part in the Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he tried to persuade Pope Vigilius to attend. The Pragmatic Sanction of 13 August 554 granted him large estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the last document of his lifetime. He died probably that year and was buried at Ariminum, where his children set up his funerary inscription. The historian James O'Donnell notes that he held the highest offices of Italy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liberius (praetorian prefect)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An imperial force under the elderly patrician <b>Liberius</b> landed in the south-east, and in the following years the Byzantines seized a coastal strip from around Cartagena to Málaga and beyond, organised as the province of Spania.</blockquote><a href="/state/byzantine-spania">Byzantine Spania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Rimini, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.45,8.77&amp;year=522" aria-label="Liguria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Liberius (praetorian prefect)"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5533 3757h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5533 3757h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.45,8.77&amp;year=522">What was here in 522 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
